No separate check — the overwrite case is just one branch of the same plain BST search loop that also locates the insertion point (`rb_insert`, src/rbtree.c:118-131):

```c
struct rb_node *parent = NULL;
struct rb_node *cur = t->root;
int cmp = 0;
while (cur != NULL) {
    cmp = strcmp(key, cur->key);
    if (cmp == 0) {
        if (t->value_free) t->value_free(cur->value);
        cur->value = value;
        return 0;
    }
    parent = cur;
    cur = (cmp < 0) ? cur->left : cur->right;
}
```

Two things guarantee it can never hit a null or stale node:

- **Null:** `cmp` is only computed, and `cur->key`/`cur->value` only dereferenced, *inside* the `while (cur != NULL)` guard. There's no path where `cur` is NULL when the `cmp == 0` branch runs — the loop condition itself is the check, not a separate one.
- **Deleted node:** there's no tombstone/soft-delete in this tree. `rb_delete` physically unlinks the node via `transplant` and `rb_free`s it (src/rbtree.c:244-282) — the slot simply stops existing in the structure. So re-inserting a previously-deleted key isn't a "resurrect a dead node" case at all; the search for that key just runs off the bottom (`cur` becomes NULL without ever hitting `cmp == 0`) and falls into the ordinary new-node allocation path below the loop, identical to inserting a key that was never there.

So "overwrite a null/deleted node" isn't a reachable state — `cmp == 0` is only ever true when `cur` is a live node currently holding that exact key.

